<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      Spring Boot 3.5.16 — the latest patch on the 3.5.x line (2026-06-25).

      History of this pin:
        3.3.5  -> failed the OWASP gate on jackson-databind 2.17.2 and
                  log4j-api 2.23.1 (real CVEs, CVSS >= 9).
        3.5.6  -> cleared those; verified against the upstream release list that
                  3.5.16 was in fact the newest 3.5.x.
        3.5.16 -> current. jackson-databind 2.21.4 and log4j-api 2.24.3 are now
                  below the critical threshold.

      Deliberately staying on 3.5.x rather than moving to 4.x: a major upgrade
      brings breaking changes (dependency baselines, config property migrations)
      that are a separate, reviewed piece of work — not something to fold into a
      security patch.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.16</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <groupId>xyz.royalbengal.shopfast</groupId>
    <artifactId>shopfast</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0</version>
    <name>ShopFast</name>
    <description>ShopFast GitOps delivery platform demo service</description>

    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <dependency-check.version>12.1.0</dependency-check.version>
        <jacoco.version>0.8.12</jacoco.version>

        <!--
          ====================================================================
          COVERAGE GATE — ENFORCING. A build below these ratios FAILS.
          ====================================================================

          Unlike security.failBuildOnCVSS below (which is deliberately in
          reporting mode for a documented upstream reason), this gate BLOCKS:
          haltOnFailure is true, and the pipeline's dedicated `coverage` stage
          invokes jacoco:check so the goal is actually executed.

          WHY THESE NUMBERS, and not a round 0.80:

          The measured scope is one class, HelloController (ShopFastApplication
          is excluded — see below). Its constructor and hello() method are both
          fully exercised by HelloControllerTest, so the honest ratio today is
          1.00 line. hello() contains no conditional, so there are no branches.

          The bar is 0.90, NOT 1.00. A gate pinned to exactly the current value
          fails on the very next line of code that is not immediately tested,
          which trains people to lower the gate rather than write the test. 0.90
          leaves headroom for one honest gap while still refusing a genuine
          regression.

          BRANCH is 0.00 for a specific, non-cosmetic reason: there are ZERO
          branches in the measured scope, and JaCoCo reports a 0/0 counter as a
          0.00 ratio, NOT as 1.00. A branch minimum above zero would therefore
          fail a build that has no branches to cover in the first place. The
          limit stays DECLARED so it begins enforcing the moment real
          conditional logic lands — at that point raise jacoco.branch.ratio.

          WHAT IS EXCLUDED, and why that is honest rather than convenient:

            ShopFastApplication — main() is never invoked by @SpringBootTest.
            Spring Test builds the ApplicationContext directly; it does not go
            through main(). The only way to "cover" it is a test that calls
            main() purely to colour a line green, which asserts nothing about
            behaviour. Excluding an uncoverable bootstrap class is the standard
            treatment; writing a fake test for it is coverage theatre.

          Nothing else is excluded, and scripts/verify-coverage-gate.sh FAILS
          the build if an exclusion ever starts covering application logic.

          REVISIT WHEN: real business logic (persistence, cart, checkout) lands.
          Then raise LINE and set BRANCH to a real value — by that point the 0/0
          problem no longer exists.
        -->
        <jacoco.line.ratio>0.90</jacoco.line.ratio>
        <jacoco.branch.ratio>0.00</jacoco.branch.ratio>

        <!--
          NVD database location — pinned so CI can CACHE it.

          dependency-check defaults this to ~/.m2/repository/org/owasp/
          dependency-check-data/<n>/, which the maven cache key (keyed on
          pom.xml) does not reliably preserve.

          Downloading the full NVD feed is ~387,000 records, and there is no
          partial mode: the whole corpus must land before a single jar can be
          inspected. Measured throughput on consecutive days ranged from ~97,000
          records/min (4m46s total) to ~2,600 records/min (~2.5h projected), and
          one run was aborted by NVD itself mid-fetch. Because that rate is not
          ours to control, the scan was moved OUT of the deploy pipeline into
          the `security-deps` workflow, which has a 3-hour budget and is the
          only writer of the shared nvd-db-* Actions cache.

          With this path pinned, that workflow restores the directory and
          dependency-check fetches only the delta since the cached snapshot.
          See docs/DEPENDENCY-SCANNING.md.
        -->
        <nvd.data.directory>${user.home}/.dependency-check-data</nvd.data.directory>

        <!--
          ====================================================================
          ACCEPTED RISK — dependency-check runs in REPORTING mode.
          Decision: project owner, 2026-09-06.
          ====================================================================

          CVSS 11 is above the maximum possible score (10.0), so no finding can
          fail the build. The scan STILL RUNS (in the `security-deps` workflow —
          see the nvd.data.directory note above) and still publishes its full
          HTML report — findings remain visible, they simply no longer block
          delivery.

          WHY: Spring Boot 3.5.16 — the newest release of this line — ships
          transitive dependencies with unpatched criticals in the HTTP stack:

            spring-core 6.2.19 / spring-web 6.2.19
              CVE-2026-59313 (9.8), CVE-2026-47892 (9.8), CVE-2026-47891 (9.8),
              CVE-2026-47890 (9.8), CVE-2026-59283 (9.1)
            tomcat-embed-core 10.1.55
              CVE-2026-65905 (9.8), CVE-2026-65637 (9.8), CVE-2026-53434 (9.1),
              CVE-2026-55276 (9.1), CVE-2026-59083 (9.1), CVE-2026-59084 (9.1),
              CVE-2026-65182 (9.1), CVE-2026-68525 (9.1)

          These are NOT false positives — each artifact matches its own correct
          CPE. There is no upstream version that resolves them today, so the
          only alternatives were "never deploy" or "suppress real criticals".
          Reporting mode was chosen over suppression because it keeps the
          findings visible and auditable rather than hiding them.

          COMPENSATING CONTROLS still enforced:
            - Trivy scans the built container image (HIGH/CRITICAL) in build_push
            - ECR scan-on-push
            - Pods run non-root, read-only rootfs, all capabilities dropped
            - The app exposes only /api/hello and three actuator endpoints

          REVISIT WHEN: upstream ships patched spring-framework / tomcat
          versions — then restore this to 9 and upgrade. Re-evaluate BEFORE this
          service handles real user data.
        -->
        <security.failBuildOnCVSS>11</security.failBuildOnCVSS>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- /actuator/health and /actuator/prometheus -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Exposes Micrometer metrics in Prometheus text format -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
            <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>shopfast</finalName>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              COVERAGE GATE. See the jacoco.* properties above for the threshold
              rationale and the exclusion justification.

              NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THESE COMMENTS: an XML comment may never
              contain a double hyphen. Pasting a raw Maven log line here (they
              are wrapped in triple hyphens) makes the POM non-parseable and
              every stage dies at "Non-parseable POM ... in comment after two
              dashes". That happened on 2026-09-08. Paraphrase log lines; do not
              paste them.

              HOW THE PIPELINE DRIVES THIS (three ways to get it wrong, all of
              which have actually happened on this project):

              1. BINDING. jacoco:check binds to the `verify` phase. `mvn test`
                 stops at `test`, which runs prepare-agent and produces
                 jacoco.exec but NEVER executes check — the gate would exist in
                 this pom and enforce nothing, silently, while the stage stayed
                 green.

              2. JOB BOUNDARIES. The gate runs in its own `coverage` stage,
                 which is a separate CI job on a separate runner. It invokes the
                 GOAL directly (`mvn jacoco:check`) rather than a lifecycle
                 phase, so it grades the execution data produced by the earlier
                 `test` stage instead of re-running the whole suite. That data
                 crosses the job boundary as the `coverage-exec-data` artifact
                 (jacoco.exec + target/classes); without BOTH, JaCoCo resolves
                 nothing and reports 0%.

              3. ***CONFIGURATION SCOPE — why <rules> and <haltOnFailure> are
                 HERE, at plugin level, and NOT inside the <execution> below.***

                 A goal invoked from the command line does NOT inherit an
                 <execution>'s <configuration>. `mvn jacoco:check` creates a
                 SYNTHETIC execution, and Maven prints its id in parentheses on
                 the goal banner:

                     jacoco:0.8.12:check (default-cli) @ shopfast

                 `default-cli`, not `jacoco-check`. That synthetic execution
                 reads ONLY the plugin-level <configuration>. With the rules
                 nested inside the named execution, the CLI invocation saw no
                 <rules> at all and the build died with:

                     The parameters 'rules' for goal
                     org.jacoco:jacoco-maven-plugin:0.8.12:check
                     are missing or invalid

                 Keeping rules/haltOnFailure at PLUGIN level means BOTH callers
                 see identical thresholds:
                   a) `mvn jacoco:check`  (the coverage stage, CI)
                   b) `mvn verify`        (a local build, via the execution below)
                 The <execution> is retained purely for the phase binding; it
                 deliberately carries no configuration of its own, so the two
                 paths can never drift apart.

                 Note this failed LOUDLY rather than silently — a missing
                 required parameter is an error, not a default. That is the good
                 case. It is the reason check 10 in the guard script now asserts
                 reachability, not just presence.

              scripts/verify-coverage-gate.sh asserts all of these, so an edit
              that collapses the gate back into `mvn test`, drops the artifact
              hand-off, or re-nests the rules inside the execution fails the
              build instead of disarming the gate. See docs/COVERAGE.md.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
                <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${jacoco.version}</version>

                <!-- PLUGIN-LEVEL configuration: read by BOTH the phase-bound
                     executions below AND by a direct `mvn jacoco:check` /
                     `mvn jacoco:report` command line. See point 3 above. -->
                <configuration>
                    <excludes>
                        <!-- Bootstrap class: main() is not invoked by
                             @SpringBootTest. See the properties block. -->
                        <exclude>xyz/royalbengal/shopfast/ShopFastApplication.class</exclude>
                    </excludes>

                    <!-- THE GATE FAILS THE BUILD. haltOnFailure=false would
                         turn it into a warning that nobody reads. -->
                    <haltOnFailure>true</haltOnFailure>

                    <rules>
                        <rule>
                            <element>BUNDLE</element>
                            <limits>
                                <limit>
                                    <counter>LINE</counter>
                                    <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                    <minimum>${jacoco.line.ratio}</minimum>
                                </limit>
                                <!--
                                  BRANCH stays declared at 0.00 while the
                                  measured scope has no branches: JaCoCo reports
                                  a 0/0 counter as 0.00, not 1.00, so any
                                  positive minimum would fail a build that has
                                  nothing to cover. Raise jacoco.branch.ratio
                                  when real conditional logic lands.
                                -->
                                <limit>
                                    <counter>BRANCH</counter>
                                    <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                                    <minimum>${jacoco.branch.ratio}</minimum>
                                </limit>
                            </limits>
                        </rule>
                    </rules>
                </configuration>

                <executions>
                    <!-- Attaches the coverage agent to the surefire JVM. Bound
                         to the default `initialize` phase, so it runs under
                         `mvn test` in the test stage. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-prepare-agent</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!-- Human-readable + machine-readable reports, uploaded as a
                         CI artifact so a gate failure is diagnosable from the
                         run itself rather than only reproducible locally.

                         The test stage invokes `jacoco:report` EXPLICITLY
                         alongside `mvn test`, because the pipeline spec has no
                         `if:` key: an upload placed after the gate could not use
                         if: always() and would be skipped on precisely the run
                         that failed. Generating the report in the always-green
                         test stage guarantees it exists for every run. The
                         `verify` binding here is retained so a plain local
                         `mvn verify` still produces the report. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-report</id>
                        <phase>verify</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>report</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>

                    <!-- Phase binding ONLY, so a local `mvn verify` enforces the
                         same gate as CI. It carries NO <configuration> on
                         purpose: the rules and haltOnFailure live at plugin
                         level above, where the command-line invocation used by
                         the coverage stage can also see them. Re-nesting them
                         here would silently restore the (default-cli) failure. -->
                    <execution>
                        <id>jacoco-check</id>
                        <phase>verify</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>check</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              Pinned here so the security-deps workflow and any local run use the
              same analyzer version and settings. Invoked by
              scripts/dependency-scan.sh.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.owasp</groupId>
                <artifactId>dependency-check-maven</artifactId>
                <version>${dependency-check.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- See the security.failBuildOnCVSS property above for the
                         accepted-risk rationale and the revisit condition. -->
                    <failBuildOnCVSS>${security.failBuildOnCVSS}</failBuildOnCVSS>
                    <!-- Cacheable NVD location — see the nvd.data.directory
                         property above for why this is pinned. -->
                    <dataDirectory>${nvd.data.directory}</dataDirectory>
                    <assemblyAnalyzerEnabled>false</assemblyAnalyzerEnabled>
                    <retireJsAnalyzerEnabled>false</retireJsAnalyzerEnabled>
                    <!--
                      Sonatype OSS Index needs its own account. Without credentials
                      it logs "Invalid credentials" for every jar and adds nothing;
                      NVD remains the authoritative source here.
                    -->
                    <ossindexAnalyzerEnabled>false</ossindexAnalyzerEnabled>
                    <!-- Both HTML (human review) and JSON (machine parsing). -->
                    <formats>
                        <format>HTML</format>
                        <format>JSON</format>
                    </formats>
                    <suppressionFiles>
                        <suppressionFile>${project.basedir}/owasp-suppressions.xml</suppressionFile>
                    </suppressionFiles>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
